    <dcterms:title>Pride and Prejudice</dcterms:title>
    <dcterms:description>Wikipedia page about this book: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pride_and_Prejudice</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:description>This title is also available as eBook #1342.</dcterms:description>
    <pgterms:marc508>E-text prepared by Greg Weeks, Jon Hurst, Mary Meehan, and the Online Distributed Proofreading Team (http://www.pgdp.net) from page images generously made available by Internet Archive (http://archive.org)</pgterms:marc508>
    <pgterms:marc520>"Pride and Prejudice" by Jane Austen is a novel published in 1813. It follows Elizabeth Bennet, who must learn to see beyond hasty judgments and superficial appearances. With five daughters and an estate that can only pass to a male heir, the Bennet family faces financial uncertainty. Marriage becomes essential for survival. When wealthy Mr. Darcy arrives in their neighborhood, his pride and Elizabeth's prejudice ignite a conflict that will challenge everything she believes about goodness, character, and love. (This is an automatically generated summary.)</pgterms:marc520>
    <pgterms:marc904>http://archive.org/stream/novelstextbasedo02austuoft#page/n23/mode/2up</pgterms:marc904>
    <pgterms:marc908>Reading ease score: 69.5 (8th &amp; 9th grade). Neither easy nor difficult to read.</pgterms:marc908>
